            {
              "id": "c3192606-4e8b-31b1-bb21-4b9d3630dae2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. The specific vulnerability in this case is that the size of the memory being allocated may not be correct. \n\nIn the provided code snippet `malloc(sizeof(char*))`, memory is being allocated for a pointer to a character, not for a character itself. This can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the programmer intended to allocate memory for a character but instead allocated memory for a pointer. \n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is important to ensure that the correct amount of memory is being allocated. If the intention is to allocate memory for a character, the code should be `malloc(sizeof(char))`. If the intention is to allocate memory for a pointer to a character, the code is correct as is.\n\nAdditionally, it is important to always check the return value of `malloc`. If `malloc` fails to allocate the requested memory, it will return a null pointer. Attempting to access memory through a null pointer will result in undefined behavior, which can lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf the intention is to allocate memory for a character:\n\n```c++\nchar* ptr = (char*) malloc(sizeof(char));\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\nIf the intention is to allocate memory for a pointer to a character:\n\n```c++\nchar** ptr = (char**) malloc(sizeof(char*));\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
