            {
              "id": "31fda8ef-76b3-3c6b-821d-75864e0f498d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In the provided code, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file name or path is manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of file operations, C++ provides the `fstream` library which is safer and more flexible than the C-style `fopen` function. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with the `ofstream` object from the `fstream` library. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ofstream fb;\nfb.open(\"b.dat\");\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `fopen` function\n\nThe fixed code requires the following library:\n\n- `fstream` for `ofstream` object\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
