            {
              "id": "274f8a8f-1db1-3048-bdb3-e627ce2e5161",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which can lead to integer overflow or underflow if the input is not properly validated or sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions that perform bounds checking or input validation. For instance, instead of `atoi()`, you can use `strtol()` or `sscanf()`, which provide error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi()` function with `strtol()` or `sscanf()`. Here is an example of how to do it with `strtol()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <cerrno>\n\nchar *end;\nlong int device_id = strtol(argv[i + 1], &end, 10);\n\nif (end == argv[i + 1] || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle conversion error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `strtol()`\n- `<cerrno>`: for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b6632b9c-7b16-3fc5-9e70-83aa9be79b2f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `localtime` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to data races if used in a multi-threaded context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions in multi-threaded applications. Instead, use their thread-safe counterparts. In this case, use `localtime_r` instead of `localtime`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <time.h>\n\ntime_t rawtime;\nstruct tm * ptm;\n\ntime(&rawtime);\nptm = localtime(&rawtime);\n\n// Replace the above localtime call with the following thread-safe version\nstruct tm result;\nptm = localtime_r(&rawtime, &result);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- time.h\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7986b1a9-6214-38ec-9ae5-bd0c7e6badfd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the function `localtime` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to data races if used in a multithreaded context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use thread-safe alternatives to `localtime`, such as `localtime_r` in POSIX compliant systems. This function requires an additional parameter, a pointer to a `struct tm`, where the result is stored.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <ctime>\n\n// ...\n\ntime_t rawtime;\nstruct tm timeinfo;\n\ntime(&rawtime);\nlocaltime_r(&rawtime, &timeinfo);\nptm = &timeinfo;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `<ctime>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a2a74b13-0342-3d9c-a9db-5b1e401877c2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function is used to determine the length of a string, but it does not include the null character at the end of the string. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` can lead to buffer over-read, causing undefined behavior, which can be exploited by attackers.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(\"--tol=\")` is used to offset the pointer `argv[i]`. If `argv[i]` is shorter than the length of `\"--tol=\"`, this can lead to reading memory out of bounds.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the strings you are working with are null-terminated. In addition, you should always check the length of the string before using functions like `strlen` to avoid reading memory out of bounds.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer way to handle this would be to use `strncmp` to check if the argument starts with `\"--tol=\"`, and then use `sscanf` to parse the floating point number. Here is a possible fix:\n\n```cpp\nif (strncmp(argv[i], \"--tol=\", strlen(\"--tol=\")) == 0) {\n    sscanf(argv[i]+strlen(\"--tol=\"), \"%lf\", &fparam);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdio>` for `sscanf`\n- `<cstring>` for `strlen` and `strncmp`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2eb62488-bfb0-3b5d-ab46-ceabc8485cc6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function is used to determine the length of a string, but it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used correctly. In this case, the `strlen` function is used to calculate the offset for the `sscanf` function, which can lead to reading from memory that is out of bounds if the length of `cparams[j]` is greater than `argv[i]`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the string you are working with is null-terminated and that you are not reading or writing past the end of the allocated memory. You should also consider using functions that limit the number of characters read or written, such as `strncpy` or `snprintf`, to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA potential fix for this vulnerability would be to check the lengths of `argv[i]` and `cparams[j]` before using them in the `sscanf` function. Here is an example of how you might do this:\n\n```cpp\nif(strlen(argv[i]) >= strlen(cparams[j])) {\n    sscanf(argv[i]+strlen(cparams[j]), \"%d\", iparams+j) != 1\n}\n```\n\nThis code checks to make sure that `argv[i]` is at least as long as `cparams[j]` before attempting to read from it. This prevents the `sscanf` function from reading past the end of `argv[i]`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `#include <stdio.h>` for the `sscanf` function.\n- `#include <string.h>` for the `strlen` function.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be available at all times."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cf7d0d3a-94dd-3252-8086-4ba6eef099b9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to calculate the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. Be careful when manipulating strings, especially when using functions like `strncpy` that do not guarantee a null-terminated string. Also, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` that take a maximum length parameter to prevent reading past the end of the string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strlen`, consider using `strnlen` which takes a maximum length parameter:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n\n// ...\n\nsize_t max_length = 100; // replace with actual maximum length\nn = strnlen(prefix, max_length);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<string.h>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "30449d4e-63eb-33be-b1d1-37ece67f1630",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of functions or methods that generate random numbers which are not suitable for security-critical operations. The `rand()` function, for example, is a weak random number generator because it is predictable and can be easily reproduced, making it unsuitable for generating secure random numbers.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is the use of `rand()` function to generate random numbers:\n\n```cpp\nrng[i] = rand() / (double)(RAND_MAX) + 1.0\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong cryptographic random number generator instead of `rand()`. In C++, you can use the `<random>` library which provides a variety of random number generators that are more secure and less predictable than `rand()`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the vulnerability sink:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(1.0, 2.0);\n\nrng[i] = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is a uniformly-distributed integer random number generator that produces non-deterministic random numbers. `std::mt19937` is a Mersenne Twister pseudo-random generator of 32-bit numbers with a state size of 19937 bits. `std::uniform_real_distribution` produces random floating-point numbers in the range [1.0, 2.0).\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "30449d4e-63eb-33be-b1d1-37ece67f1630"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "810bcc35-f953-3c30-9caa-f815f38355fe",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of functions or methods that generate random numbers which are not suitable for security-critical operations. The `rand()` function, for example, is a weak random number generator because it is predictable and can be easily reproduced, making it unsuitable for generating secure random numbers.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is the use of `rand()` function to generate random numbers:\n\n```cpp\nrng[i] = rand() / (double)(RAND_MAX) + 1.0\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong cryptographic random number generator instead of `rand()`. In C++, you can use the `<random>` library which provides a variety of random number generators that are more secure and less predictable than `rand()`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the vulnerability sink:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(1.0, 2.0);\n\nrng[i] = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is a uniformly-distributed integer random number generator that produces non-deterministic random numbers. `std::mt19937` is a Mersenne Twister pseudo-random generator of 32-bit numbers with a state size of 19937 bits. `std::uniform_real_distribution` produces random floating-point numbers in the range [1.0, 2.0).\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "810bcc35-f953-3c30-9caa-f815f38355fe"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d03b77d8-b339-3287-b98c-e06190b1de79",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, \"Avoid Using Weak or Non-Cryptographic Random Number Generators\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or non-cryptographic random number generators that can lead to predictable or easily guessable values. This can be exploited by attackers to predict the sequence of random numbers and compromise the security of the system.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use strong cryptographic random number generators. In C++, you can use the `<random>` library which provides a variety of random number generators that are suitable for cryptographic use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to use the `<random>` library to generate a strong random number:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nint main() {\n    std::random_device rd;  // Will be used to obtain a seed for the random number engine\n    std::mt19937 gen(rd()); // Standard mersenne_twister_engine seeded with rd()\n    std::uniform_int_distribution<> distrib(1, 6);\n\n    int i = distrib(gen); // Generate a random number\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is a uniformly-distributed integer random number generator that produces non-deterministic random numbers. `std::mt19937` is a Mersenne Twister pseudo-random generator of 32-bit numbers with a state size of 19937 bits. `std::uniform_int_distribution` produces random integers in a range [a, b].\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "d03b77d8-b339-3287-b98c-e06190b1de79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b2aed774-3100-36c1-ba4b-0801ccb474f6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, \"Avoid Using Weak or Non-Cryptographic Random Number Generators\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or non-cryptographic random number generators that can lead to predictable or easily guessable values. This can be exploited by attackers to predict the sequence of random numbers and compromise the security of the system.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use strong cryptographic random number generators. In C++, you can use the `<random>` library which provides a variety of random number generators that are suitable for cryptographic use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to use the `<random>` library to generate a strong random number:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nint main() {\n    std::random_device rd;  // Will be used to obtain a seed for the random number engine\n    std::mt19937 gen(rd()); // Standard mersenne_twister_engine seeded with rd()\n    std::uniform_int_distribution<> distrib(1, 6);\n\n    int i = distrib(gen); // Generate a random number\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is a uniformly-distributed integer random number generator that produces non-deterministic random numbers. `std::mt19937` is a Mersenne Twister pseudo-random generator of 32-bit numbers with a state size of 19937 bits. `std::uniform_int_distribution` produces random integers in a range [a, b].\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
